# What may be a binary, or a blob in a source file, in this repository, and why.
# Read by tools/provenance-check.py (the common repository's checker). Format:  STATUS  GLOB  reason
#   open     redistributable
#   pending  known, not settled: the check passes with a warning
#   vendor   proprietary: the check fails until the file leaves the tree
#   flash    a script allowed to write to the phone's storage, by a person's hand
# The first line that matches a path decides. A file that no line explains fails the check.

# --- built from this repository -----------------------------------------------------------------------
open     prebuilt/mach.development.bcm2837        the kernel, built from Apple's open xnu-8796.121.2 plus xnu/hakios.patch and xnu/files: APSL-2.0, corresponding source here and at Apple
open     prebuilt/loader.efi                      the boot loader, built from xnuloader/loader.c: MIT
open     prebuilt/userland/*                      our programs and libraries, built from userland/src: MIT
open     devices/poco-x5-pro/prebuilt/BootShim.bin   built from the shim of Mu-Silicium with our change (uefi/mu-silicium.patch): BSD-2-Clause-Patent

# --- a description of the hardware ------------------------------------------------------------------------
pending  devices/poco-x3-pro/prebuilt/device-tree.dtb      the POCO X3 Pro's device tree as Mu-Silicium ships it (Resources/DTBs/vayu.dtb), compiled from Linux sources that are GPL-2.0; confirm the headers, or have each user pull it from their own phone
pending  devices/poco-x5-pro/prebuilt/device-tree.dtb      the POCO X5 Pro's device tree, compiled from Linux sources that are GPL-2.0; confirm the headers, or have each user pull it from their own phone
pending  devices/poco-x5-pro/uefi/files/Resources/DTBs/redwood.dtb   the same device tree, for the UEFI build

# --- the touch controller's firmware, compiled into a driver ---------------------------------------------
pending  xnu/files/bsd/dev/hakios.focaltech_fw.h   FocalTech's firmware as a C array, from a blob on the POCO X5 Pro: the kernel (hakios.goodix.c) includes it, so it cannot simply be removed; to be loaded from the phone at run time like the rest of the firmware

# --- proprietary: Qualcomm's and Xiaomi's, taken out of the phone's own UEFI; not for a public tree ---------
vendor   devices/*/uefi/files/Binaries/*/*.efi      UEFI drivers extracted from the vendor's xbl firmware: each user extracts their own (UEFIReader)
vendor   devices/*/prebuilt/SILICIUM_UEFI.fd         a built UEFI image: carries those drivers

# --- Apple's files: made on the user's machine from their own IPSW, never committed -------------------------
vendor   test24-patches/F10-patches/appex/*         Apple's LocalStorageFileProvider.appex: make-appex.py makes it from the user's own system image

# --- everything that can write to the phone's storage: a short list, each limited in code --------------------
flash    xnuloader/loader.c                       the loader writes its own log (a few KB) into the phone's `logdump` partition at the end of a run and, only with ufs_write=1, into the partition `darwin`; it REFUSES every other write (BLOCK_OP_WRITE: "not darwin ... never")
flash    tools/userdata-carve.py                  prints the dd commands that cut the `darwin` partition off the end of Android's userdata (a new GPT); a person runs them once, by hand, under Android, and only for the optional persistence; the default run needs no such partition
